    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The «-en» ending of the masculine accusative",
     "texto": [
      "When a masculine noun goes from being the subject (nominative) to receiving the verb's direct action (accusative), its determiners add the ending **-en**:",
      "**der** → **den** (Ich frage den Bruder = I ask the brother).",
      "**ein** → **einen** (Ich habe einen Bruder = I have a brother).",
      "**kein** → **keinen** (Ich habe keinen Sohn = I don't have a son).",
      "**mein** → **meinen** (Sie fragt meinen Vater = She asks my father).",
      "**dein** → **deinen** (Hast du deinen Bruder gefragt? = Have you asked your brother?).",
      "The feminine and neuter, on the other hand, don't change at all:",
      "Feminine: **eine Schwester** (subject) → **eine Schwester** (accusative).",
      "Neuter: **ein Kind** (subject) → **ein Kind** (accusative)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
